and I also added one in  order to have a minimal confidence even  if R is equal to zero  now we are trying to minimize the  following cost function which is the  confidence multiplied by the square  difference between the preference and  the dot product of the user and item  vectors and again adding the  regularization term  this cost function depends on M and N  where m is the number of users and N is  the number of items and even for typical  data sets M multiplies n can easily be  can easily reach a few billion and the  regular optimization method such as  stochastic gradient descentes GD  wouldn't work well for this type of data  therefore in the paper they offer to use  an alternative optimization method which  is the alternate list squared algorithm  ALS  this method suggests fixing each factor  at a time and if we are fixing the user  factors or the item factors we can  calculate the global minimum  it means that we are alternating between  recomputing the user factors and the  item factors and when fixing either the  user of the item factors the cost  function became quadratic so its Global  minimum can be easily computed and each  step is guaranteed to lower the value of  the cost function  the full mathematical calculation of  demand of this method is out of the  scope of that presentation and you can  read about it later  so now after solving everything and  calculated the matrices we can finally  move to the recommendation process  in Python there is an implementation of  this algorithm in the implicit Library  this Library provides first python  implementation of several different  popular recommendation algorithms for  implicit feedback data sets and one of  them is the alternating list scores  algorithm as described in the paper  in order to install it you just need to  do prep installing place it and you'll  be able to import it and use it  for using the ALS algorithm you need to  initialize the model first using  implicit.als.alternate English square  and then choose the number of factors  which is the number of latent features  to compute then choose the  regularization factor to use and also  choose the number of ALS iterations to  use when fitting the data  later we need to create the confidence  Matrix so we are choosing the alpha  value which here is 40. and multiply it  by multiply it by the sparse user item  Matrix  then we are fitting the data to the  model using this confidence metrics and  creating our model  and now that we have the trained model  we can move to the recommendation  we can recommend items for the user  using the recommend function  we are choosing the user we want to  recommend the items to and we are also  choosing them the number of items to  recommend and the function also needs  the original user item Matrix in order  to filter out the already used items  the function calculates the top and best  recommended items for the user and  returns a list of items like this and  the corresponding score for each item  for example item number 566 received a  score of 0.86 Which is higher than the  item 166 received which is 0.7  one more interesting function is the  rank items function where you can choose  a user and a set of items you want to  rank and the model will calculate their  score and rank the items according to  the preference of the user  again the function Returns the IDS of  the items along with the score  as you can see the scores aren't always  between 0 and 1. it happens because  there is no explicit scale here the  scores are used only for ranking then  sorting the recommendations where the  higher score means the most recommended  item  when calculating the dot product of the  users and the item vectors we are trying  to make it as close as possible to the  real preferences but since there are no  boundaries it can take any possible  value  by finding the optimal parameters we are  pushing those values close to 0 and 1  but when adding the regularization term  and the confidence weight those  additional factors may push them in  different directions so we don't always  end up with zero to one values  so now that we know how recommendation  system works and how to use it properly  we can move to the real problem where we  are here to talk about which is keeping  our data safe and analyzing our  databases access patterns  our domain is databases access and the  actions we observe are users who access  to tables and extract or update the data  in it  so our objects are users and tables and  the relationship is that the users are  consuming the tables so the tables are  the items  users are accessing to table using  queries so we are looking on the amount  of queries each user performed on each  table and that would be our implicit  feedback  in general high amount of queries on the  tables means that the user liked the  table it's relevant for him but if there  is a low amount of queries on a table it  means that the user dislikes the table  or as we discussed few minutes ago it  may be because he needed it only for a  few times  in addition if the user didn't access a  table at all it could be because this  table is irrelevant to him or because he  didn't knew about it or didn't have the  chance to use it yet  and again that's exactly why in the  algorithm we are using they added the  confidence value on the user preference  and in the cases where we don't have  many interactions with the table the  confidence will be low  so the problem we are trying to solve is  to solve here is the is finding the  anomalies in those actions of accesses  to tables  basically it is events of unexpected or  unauthorized access to tables for  example if someone is working on a  project of Type X is usually accessing  tables that related to this project X  because that's what he needs to perform  his job  but what if what does what if suddenly  is accessing all tables which doesn't  relate to his project and doesn't relate  to her to any one of his team members  project and might contain sensitive data  that he doesn't supposed to have access  to at all  for example we don't want someone from  the user interface team in Netflix we'll  access the customer's data  this data isn't relevant for user  interface and there is no excuse for  this user to access this data  so we need to prevent those events and  keep our data safe  we should create an alert for those kind  of actions so we'll be able to protect  this data and make sure it doesn't  exfiltrate it out of the organization  but our recommendation system related to  all of it and how it can help us solve  our problem  as we mentioned the Bob our goal is  detecting anomalies in the user's usage  patterns detect when the user is  behaving differently from the way it's  supposed to behave and Performing  actions that doesn't fit with his  previous actions  usually in recommendation systems we are  looking for items that the user is  supposed to like and recommend it to him  but here in this case we are looking for  the outliers which is the opposite  direction of recommendations the items  that the user is unlikely to use  and that's what I want you to take from  that presentation that sometimes you  need to switch your algorithm and use it  in an opposite way in order to solve  your problem  for example in the movies domain instead  of focusing on movies the user would  like we are looking for the least  expected movies with the lowest  probability that this user would watch  think about me with the French  documentary movie we talked about I want  to know when there is a change in the  user's Behavior  so in our solution we are looking for  those unrecommended items that the user  is not supposed to use  and the alert One is using those items  and might cause dangerous consequences  to the organization  so we need to build a recommendation  model based on the databases access logs  we need to count the amount of queries  each user performed on each table and  this will be our implicit data which is  the sparse user item Matrix  we are building the ALS implicit model  using the python Library we are setting  the values for the regularization term  the regularization factor the number of  ALS iterations the number of latent  factors and the value of alpha it is of  the value of the scaling Factor Alpha  we decided to train the model using six  months of queries data so we'll have  enough data for training the model  we also decided to count new access to a  table only if the user is accessing the  table using different queries on the  same day  it means that if the user is using the  same query for several times in the same  day we will count it only once but in  the next day it will be count again as a  new query  we did that because we wanted to remove  automated queries that runs the same  query over and over again  and create a really high amount of  access to tables it creates a lot of  noise in the data and we wanted to  reduce that noise and make the model as  close as possible to the real access  patterns  in addition we also set a threshold for  minimum access for users and tables and  we filtered out real users  everyone is good  um  okay so we filtered out their user who  accessed only a few tables and also  tables that almost no users have access  to  so now we have a trained model that can  calculate recommendations but how can we  know that it is actually working  in order to prove the accuracy of the  model we perform two experiments where  we tested the output of the model  in the first experiment we simulated  multiple accesses to tables and check  the score the model gave to each item to  each axis we created a test set of 500  events of user to access to tables and  we decided to consider for the test set  only events of users who access to a  tables for more than 10 times so we'll  be able to be sure that this table is  indeed relevant for the user  we chose those events because we are  dealing with implicit data and if we are  choosing the user who access to a table  only once it could be because we access  it by mistake and it's irrelevant to him  and we wanted to be sure that we are  testing only known preferences  so we turn the model using the access  data and then we calculated this course  for the test set and plotted the results  on The Following histogram  almost all of the observation received  high score around one which indicates  the accuracy of the model since it means  that the model is predicting the scores  correctly according to the real  preference of the user because we chose  for the test set only items that we know  for sure that the user actually liked  because they used it for more than 10  times  the second experiment we made is based  on the assumption that it in the world  of databases access similar users who  works on the same team or works in the  same types of project will access the  same groups of table that related to  their project or to their team  and the outliers are when someone is  accessing the tables that he didn't  access before and it doesn't relate to  any other of his previous tables or to  any of the tables of his similar User  Group  so in order to simulate good and bad  access to tables we are choosing 500  random users and for each other we are  choosing two unseen tables that this  user never accessed before  the first table is taken from the group  of tables that each team already  accessed and used and the second table  is taken from the group of tables that  no one from his team accessed before  now we are feeding those scenarios into  the model and we are calculating this  cause  in order to evaluate the results we  calculated the kernel density of the  estimation of this course and as can be  seen in the graph the blue tables which  are the tables taken from the same team  receive dire scores than the red tables  that was taken from other users  you can say the separation between the  blue and delay tables and 