ther since they are working  on the same type of data  obviously there are some challenges in  this method for example possible  challenge could be that that we have  different level of sensitivity for each  table and that each table should be  handled in a different way  we can identify the set of sensitive  data and give it smaller threshold so  we'll be able to control the sensitivity  of that access  and tables that contains more sensitive  data will get more focused while tables  with regular data will get higher  threshold  this way we can play with the threshold  in order to control the noisiness of the  alerts according to the sensitivity of  the data  another challenge could be changes  inside the organization employees were  moving from one team to another changes  of responsibilities and so on  this could confuse the model because now  we have users who access to tables that  are far from their previous tables  we can solve this problem by defining  some Grace time when we are learning the  new vectors again and canceling the  alert for dollars from the team for a  while until we are learning the new  updated vectors  for conclusion if there is one thing I  want you to take from that presentation  is that sometimes you need to go the  opposite way in order to solve your  problem and to get to the right  direction  I presented the idea of using  recommendation systems for the problem  of anomaly detection  we talked about recommendation systems  in general and specifically about  implicit feedback and the ALS algorithm  later we discussed about how can we use  this algorithm for databases access  problem and about using the opposite  direction the direction of the  recommendation process in order to find  anomalies instead of recommendations  now we can detect unauthorized access to  our data to our tables get a score for  each new access and alert or block that  event accordingly  and one you once you'll Embrace this way  of thinking and implementing the 